WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.340
In the whole of Russian history, there have
been a mere handful of men to earn the nickname

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.340
“the great”.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.420
But there has only ever been one woman.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.800
Catherine the Great was an empress who defied
both her circumstances and the opinions of

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.809
those around her to shape an entire era.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:18.869
Born into a minor German family in the first
half of the 18th Century, she found herself

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:23.910
thrust into the spotlight when she married
Peter III of Russia aged only 16.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.420
But rather than being a pushover, Catherine
used her new position to grab the imperial

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.630
crown for herself.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:37.570
Empress of Russia from 1762 to 1796, Catherine’s
reign spanned global conflicts and revolutions,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.120
uprisings and royal assassinations.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:45.790
But she never lost sight of her goal: to forge
a modern Russia from the ideals of the Enlightenment.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.340
A philosopher empress to some, an amoral,
sex-obsessed harlot to others, this is the

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.910
life of one of Russia’s greatest rulers.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:06.610
An Unlikely Ruler
To say Catherine the Great’s path to the

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.110
throne was an unlikely one is a little bit
like saying Siberia gets a bit chilly in winter.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:16.270
At the moment she was born Sophie Friederike
Auguste on May 2, 1729, the likelihood of

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:21.820
Catherine becoming ruler of Russia was up
there with me one day becoming emperor of

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:22.820
Japan.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.020
By the way Tokyo, if the new guy doesn’t
work out, do give me a call.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.190
Young Sophie - who we’re just gonna start
calling Catherine right now to avoid confusion

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.190
- didn’t have a drop of Russian blood in
her.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.890
Rather, her father was a minor German prince,
while her mother Joanna was a slightly-less

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.850
minor aristocrat who had ideas above her station.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.909
And those ideas emphatically did not involve
having a daughter.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:49.820
From the moment Joanna discovered she’d
pumped out not a useful male heir but a useless

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.829
girl, she treated Catherine less like a human
and more like a receptacle for all her disappointments.

00:01:55.829 --> 00:01:59.899
Joanna called her daughter useless, ugly,
told her she was worthless.

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:04.520
It probably didn’t help that Joanna herself
was a mere 17 when she became a mother, and

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.740
that her arranged marriage to Catherine’s
much-older dad hadn’t exactly been a fairytale.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:13.740
It probably also didn’t help that Catherine
went out of her way to confirm Joanna’s

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:14.740
fears.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:15.740
She was clumsy.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:16.740
Accident prone.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.270
There’s a story that she once managed to
dislocate her spine just by sneezing.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:26.050
Yet, even amid all these excursions into klutz
territory, it was clear there was something

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.190
more to Catherine than Joanna gave her credit
for.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.390
When she was just four, she met the future
king Frederick the Great.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.270
Told to kiss his hem as a mark of respect,
she instead demanded the royal bow down to

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.830
her so she could reach.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.820
Frederick was so tickled by this that he never
forgot the confident young girl.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.810
Yet it would be another encounter that really
defined Catherine’s childhood.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:51.130
A grandson of Peter the Great, Peter Ulrich
was a mere 11 when he and 10-year old Catherine

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.920
met, and about as far from his famous grandfather
as can be imagined.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:55.920
While Peter the Great had ruled Russia, Peter
Ulrich was German through and through.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.870
While Peter the Great had been a dashing warrior-reformer,
his grandson was sickly and neurotic.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.010
He was also deeply damaged.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.720
Left in the care of a sadistic tutor, Peter
Ulrich had spent his life being beaten, tortured,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.530
and humiliated by the adult meant to care
for him.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:14.991
He was made to stand at the dining table,
a picture of a donkey hung around his neck.

00:03:14.991 --> 00:03:18.130
Made to endure savage whippings for the tiniest
infraction.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.960
As our video unfolds today, Peter Ulrich is
going to take a role best essentially as the

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:22.960
villain.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.500
As he does so, just remember that the abuse
he suffered as a child is at least partly

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:28.500
to blame.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.040
Despite their differences, Catherine and Peter
hit it off during their first meeting, becoming

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.150
not exactly friends, but maybe friendly acquaintances.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.200
Still, there’s no way Catherine could’ve
known just what fate had in store for them.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.980
No way she could’ve know that this sickly,
troubled lad was the future emperor she was

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.700
destined to overthrow.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:55.060
The Empire of Ice
On the frozen night of December 5, 1741, a

00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:00.040
group of guards marched into the bedchamber
of Russia’s infant emperor Ivan VI and arrested

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.620
him and his mother, Anna Leopoldovna.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.930
By the morning of December 6, both mother
and child had been locked away and Moscow

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.210
had a new ruler.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:12.840
The daughter of Peter the Great, Empress Elizabeth
was the Imelda Macros of the 18th Century,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.790
a woman obsessed with clothes, spectacle,
and living the good life on the state budget.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.680
She surrounded herself with pomp and fawning
courtiers, and forbade anyone but herself

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.970
from wearing pink.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:28.260
But for our story today, the defining feature
of Elizabeth was that she was childless.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.510
Not long after her coup against Ivan, Elizabeth
decided to bag herself an heir by inviting

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.090
Peter Ulrich to Moscow to be her son.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:41.010
Two years later, in 1743, a letter landed
in the hands of Catherine’s mother, Joanna.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:45.660
From Empress Elizabeth, it invited Joanna
to come and see Moscow along with her darling

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.660
daughter.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.740
Shortly after, another letter arrived, this
one from Frederick the Great, filled with

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.060
subtle hints that the Prussian king could
help Joanna marry her daughter off.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.680
Suddenly, it looked like Catherine was going
to Russia.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.730
For Joanna, this was the fairytale she’d
been waiting for her whole married life.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.840
She immediately set about spending the money
Frederick had forwarded on fancy dresses and

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:05.840
heaps of jewelry.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.870
Not for Catherine, but for herself.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.230
Joanna wanted to look her absolute best on
the day she dumped her useless daughter on

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.320
some unsuspecting Russian prince.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.590
By winter, 1743, Joanna and Catherine were
on the road to St Petersburg.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.720
If you’re wondering how Catherine felt about
being whisked away to marry some dude she

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.510
barely knew, the answer is that it didn’t
matter.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.130
This was Europe in the 18th century.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.370
Arranged marriages were the norm in the upper
classes.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:37.430
And, aged only 14, Catherine had grown into
a very desirable bachelorette.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.430
She was witty.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.430
Confident.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:40.430
Disarming.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:43.660
Although she wasn’t a legendary beauty,
she was already fighting off marriage proposals

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.460
- not least from Joanna’s own brother.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.460
Awkward.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.570
Finally, after a very long, very cold journey,
Catherine and Joanna arrived in Moscow.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:57.600
The imperial welcome they received was so
big, so lavish that it left no-one in doubt

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.200
about Catherine’s future role in the empire.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.830
Now all she had to do was actually meet Peter
again after all these years.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:12.370
A Match Made in Hell
If Catherine had grown up to be a charmer,

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:17.150
Peter Ulrich had grown up to be the exact
opposite; a miserable black hole that sucked

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:19.510
all the joy out any room.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.810
The boy hated Russia.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:25.070
He hated Elizabeth, he hated Moscow, and he
hated the fact he wasn’t in his beloved

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:26.070
Prussia.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.990
At first, the arrival of the German Catherine
was a relief.

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.450
Finally, someone who could understand his
misery!

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:35.081
But while Peter’s idea of a good time was
writing fan letters to Frederick the Great

00:06:35.081 --> 00:06:40.890
and discussing how superior bratwurst was
to borscht, Catherine’s was very different.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:44.389
From the moment she arrived in Russia, the
teenage girl had fallen in love.

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:49.870
The Russia of the 1740s was a distant, mysterious
place, almost entirely rural, with only a

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:50.930
handful of cities.

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:55.730
The religion was not the Lutheranism Catherine
had grown up with, but the exotic Orthodox

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.290
rites of Eastern Christianity.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:00.050
For this girl from Germany, it was an alien
land.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:02.700
A place of endless, fascinating contrasts.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.450
And, much to Peter’s annoyance, Catherine
was determined to fit in.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:10.840
She began studying Russian, began her conversion
to Orthodoxism.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:15.330
By the time she debuted at court on her 15th
birthday, the upper classes were ready to

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.330
love her.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:21.010
It helped that Catherine could be just as
charming in Moscow as at home.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:25.740
She always had a killer quip ready, or a well-thought
out compliment to disarm strangers with.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.050
She even got a reputation for treating her
servants as kindly as her upper crust friends,

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.540
something of a big deal at the time.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:36.169
Before long, Elizabeth had bestowed the title
Grand Duchess upon her, along with the name

00:07:36.169 --> 00:07:38.980
Catherine (or Yekaterina in Russian).

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:43.010
But while the empress seemed to have made
a good choice in Catherine, she’d made a

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:45.940
much worse one in her heir.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:50.150
As Catherine grew more popular at court, Peter
grew crueler towards her.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:53.561
He told her he was going to beat her once
they were married, as that was the only way

00:07:53.561 --> 00:07:55.400
to control a woman.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.330
Started getting drunk and verbally abusing
her.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:01.230
But as bad as Peter was already, he was about
to get so much worse.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.720
Not long before their wedding, Peter came
down with a case of smallpox, a disfiguring

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.560
disease we’d really advise against Googling.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.180
It took him six weeks to recover.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.320
When he finally did, his face was so scarred
that Catherine couldn’t bring herself to

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:13.400
look at him.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.910
Bitter, angry, and miserable, Peter retreated
from the world, locking himself in his rooms

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:21.650
and refusing to do anything but dress his
servants in Prussian army uniforms and play

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:22.650
soldiers.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.990
He only really emerged for the lavish wedding
on August 21, 1745.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.820
But even then, his bitterness got the better
of him.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.700
Early in the evening, Catherine was sent by
Elizabeth to the marital bed to await Peter

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.270
and get on with the job of producing heirs.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.870
Instead, Catherine lay there for hours in
the dark, waiting for her new husband.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:43.200
When Peter finally turned up, he was so drunk
he simply collapsed.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.310
Not a word.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:45.420
Not a kiss.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.420
Not even a joke.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:51.250
Just one unhappy teenager passed out on the
floor, and another unhappy teen likely wondering

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.260
if this marriage had ruined her life.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.750
Well, if Catherine ever did wonder that, she
was wrong.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:01.740
As toxic as her marriage was, it would wind
up making her into a figure remembered by

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:02.740
history.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:06.840
She just had to survive the next few years
first.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:14.250
Intimacy and Intrigue
By late 1745, Elizabeth’s enthusiasm for

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.950
Catherine had distinctly cooled.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:19.460
There still hadn’t been any royal heirs,
so Elizabeth wrote Catherine a stern note

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.120
reminding her she better get knocked up fast.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.680
But there was very little Catherine could
do about it.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:28.910
By now, Peter was actively avoiding sex, preferring
to play with his toy soldiers and live in

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:30.150
a fantasy world.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:34.040
One evening Catherine came into their room
to find him putting a rat he’d caught on

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:35.040
trial.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.570
When she asked him what he was doing, he declared
the rat guilty and sliced off its head.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:43.050
In the wake of this incident it’s perhaps
not surprising that Catherine started an affair.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.020
Today, Catherine’s affairs are legendary.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.670
But we’ll come to all that.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:51.390
For now, just know she started sleeping with
a guy called Sergei Saltykov, possibly in

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.590
an attempt to get pregnant, and possibly with
Peter’s knowledge.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:59.600
Yet, try as she might, Catherine just couldn’t
become the baby-producing machine Elizabeth

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:00.600
wanted her to be.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.692
Oh, she got pregnant all right.

00:10:01.692 --> 00:10:02.692
Multiple times.

00:10:02.692 --> 00:10:04.580
But she always miscarried.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.130
Before anyone knew what was happening, nine
years had passed, and Catherine was still

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.130
not a mother.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.540
That finally changed on October 1, 1754.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:16.399
That day, baby Paul was born at the end of
a labor best described as “arduous.”

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:20.330
The moment the baby was out, Elizabeth came
in and snatched it away to raise herself.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:26.500
Catherine, for her part, was left lying alone
in her own blood for hours, too weak to move.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.860
With her heir-producing duties now over, Catherine
entered a new, depressing stage in her life

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.070
at the Russian court.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.920
Now the baby was here, everyone just started
ignoring her.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.250
They held a party to celebrate Paul’s birth
while Catherine was still convalescing.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:42.830
All she got was a drunk Peter sticking his
head in the door and muttering something like

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.641
“err, well done,” before going back to
the party.

00:10:45.641 --> 00:10:49.580
Had Catherine been another woman, it’s possible
her story would’ve ended here, finally beaten

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:51.460
down by her awful new family.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:53.079
But this is Catherine we’re talking about.

00:10:53.079 --> 00:10:55.750
A woman who has “the Great” in her name.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:11:00.500
Rather than give up, she spent her convalescence
reading Voltaire and plotting her return to

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:01.500
court.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.740
On Peter’s birthday, she finally managed
it.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:08.180
Kitted out in the finest regalia, Catherine
crashed the party, inviting herself in unannounced

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.890
and restyling herself “mother of the heir”.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:15.630
With breathtaking confidence, she casually
snubbed those who’d ignored her after her

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:16.630
labor.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:20.690
Dropped deliciously poisonous witticisms about
anyone who tried to stand up to her.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:24.180
From that point on, Catherine would never
let herself get isolated again.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:28.600
She cozied up to the British ambassador, accepting
bribes in return for information, bribes she

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.020
used to buy herself a network of informants.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:35.810
As the 1750s swept by, Catherine began buttering
up people in positions of power.

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:39.810
Leaders of army units, aristocrats, anyone
who might one day be useful.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:44.540
By the end of the decade, she’d even taken
two important lovers: Stanisław Poniatowski,

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:49.890
the future king of Poland, and Grigory Orlov,
the man who’d make her empress.

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:53.970
But, powerful as she now was, Catherine was
still vulnerable.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:58.710
When she gave birth to her second child, Anna,
Peter came and watched the whole thing, drunk

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:01.750
out his skull, declaring that he could do
anything to her.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:03.480
It must’ve been a chilling thing to hear.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.180
It also steeled Catherine’s resolve.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.959
If she ever wanted peace, she was going to
have to get rid of her husband once and for

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:17.110
all.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:19.740
The Battle for Power
In 1756, a war broke out that engulfed half

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:20.860
the world.

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:25.420
Known as the Seven Years’ War it involved
most of the European powers, including pitting

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:28.820
Russia against Prussia
But bad as the Seven Years’ War was, it

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:32.149
was nothing compared to the battle brewing
in the Russian court.

00:12:32.149 --> 00:12:38.750
By 1762, Peter had begun a public affair with
Elizaveta Vorontsova, a coarse woman of coarser

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:39.750
manners.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:43.050
On top of that, he was parading around wearing
Prussian uniforms and telling everyone they

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:45.220
shouldn’t be fighting Frederick the Great.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:48.830
He was even promising Catherine that, as soon
as he became emperor, he would have her shaved

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:50.560
bald and thrown in a convent.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.260
It was a threat that was about to become very
real.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:56.980
On January 5, 1762, Empress Elizabeth breathed
her last.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:13:00.680
In the wake of her death, Peter was elevated
to the throne as Peter III.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:05.250
One of his first acts was to spend Elizabeth’s
funeral procession openly celebrating her

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:06.250
death.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:08.800
His second act was to make his popular, unwanted
wife suffer.

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:13.100
In the months that followed, Peter III began
inflicting humiliation after humiliation on

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:14.100
Catherine.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:15.100
He forced her to personally award medals to
his mistress Elizaveta, dragged her out in

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:18.450
public and tried to make her confess that
their son Paul was illegitimate.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.339
But it wasn’t just Catherine Peter was getting
his vengeance on.

00:13:21.339 --> 00:13:22.990
For so long, he’d hated Russia.

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:26.800
And now he was in charge, he was going to
dismantle the whole damn country.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.240
Church property was stripped and given to
Lutheran ministeries.

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.280
Army units were forced to drink toasts to
Frederick the Great.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:37.020
Everything became pro-Prussian, anti-Russian,
and very anti-Catherine.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:41.360
For Catherine, those months must’ve been
a nightmare.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.519
Luckily, she was about to wake up.

00:13:43.519 --> 00:13:48.060
That summer, July 8, Catherine’s lover Grigory
Orlov abruptly came to her and basically said

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.130
“let’s throw this asshole over.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.130
Right now.”

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:52.460
And so they did.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:56.670
The coup was organized by Orlov with the help
of his brothers and a guy called Grigory Potemkin,

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.630
who we’re gonna come back to soon.

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:01.519
It was simple and bloodless.

00:14:01.519 --> 00:14:05.050
Dressed in a male uniform, Catherine rode
out to meet army units loyal to her.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:08.839
As they joined, more declared their loyalty,
until it was clear no-one in Moscow stood

00:14:08.839 --> 00:14:09.839
against her.

00:14:09.839 --> 00:14:14.209
Finally, on July 9, 1762, Catherine was declared
Empress Catherine II.

00:14:14.209 --> 00:14:16.180
And that was that.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.519
Well, almost.

00:14:17.519 --> 00:14:21.510
Just outside Moscow, Peter was drilling some
of his troops when word came in of the coup.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:24.080
But it wasn’t just news that was traveling
in Peter’s direction.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:26.220
Catherine was riding out at the head of an
army.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:30.930
So Peter did the only thing he could: he abdicated
and threw himself on his wife’s mercy.

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.240
Apparently, he hoped to be sent into exile
in Prussia.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:37.660
But Catherine was all like “nah,” and
had him placed under house arrest.

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.899
8 days later, Peter was dead.

00:14:39.899 --> 00:14:43.710
The historical record isn’t clear on what
killed poor, damaged Peter Ulrich.

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:45.650
Officially, it was a nasty bout of hemorrhoids.

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:48.870
Unofficially, it’s thought he was strangled
on the orders of Orlov.

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:50.890
Whatever the truth, the result was the same.

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:56.130
On September 22, 1762, Catherine was crowned
empress of Russia in a lavish ceremony at

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:57.130
the Kremlin.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.460
The anonymous girl from Germany had finally
done it.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:03.250
She’d survived the intrigues of the Russian
court, survived her marriage to a madman,

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:07.490
and smashed through the glass ceiling as effectively
as a tomahawk missile.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:09.620
Now came the hard part.

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:17.899
An Instruction for Change
In 1767, Catherine sent her legislative committee

00:15:17.899 --> 00:15:19.399
a simple Instruction.

00:15:19.399 --> 00:15:23.190
Known as the Instruction of Catherine the
Great, it set out her vision for the future.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.740
Under her watch, Russia would conform to the
ideals of the Enlightenment.

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:29.890
Things like innocent until proven guilty would
become law.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:31.380
The common good would be emphasized.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:34.300
The country’s 8 million serfs would be considered
people.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:38.820
To our ears, this can sound a little “well,
duh.”

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:41.399
But in 18th Century Russia it was a bombshell.

00:15:41.399 --> 00:15:43.810
Even other European powers were aghast.

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:47.779
France actually banned copies of the Instruction
to avoid a revolution.

00:15:47.779 --> 00:15:50.820
Yeah, good luck with that.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:55.320
But while Catherine’s ideas were revolutionary,
her implementation of them was not.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:59.519
Rather than act like good little Enlightenment
thinkers, her committee fell to squabbling.

00:15:59.519 --> 00:16:02.750
Then war broke out and the whole idea was
completely forgotten.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:08.950
The Russo-Turkish War decimated Catherine’s
domestic agenda between 1768 and 1774, but

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:12.260
at least it shored up her power, helped by
the capture of Crimea.

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:16.959
Still, by the time the dust had settled, Catherine
was 12 years into her rule and had accomplished

00:16:16.959 --> 00:16:19.079
only a fraction of what she wanted to do.

00:16:19.079 --> 00:16:21.840
So she dropped any pretence of governing by
committee.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:25.760
From now on, she was going to force her reforms
through by personal decree.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:27.870
And what reforms they were.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:32.200
Catherine opened the first schools for orphans,
the first hospitals for the destitute.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:36.420
She encouraged girls into education, at a
time when most households valued women somewhere

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:40.110
below horses and about equal with good footwear.

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:42.589
City governments were reformed to better serve
the people.

00:16:42.589 --> 00:16:47.290
The police were transformed from an instrument
for repression into one that at least nominally

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:48.680
helped ordinary folk.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:52.720
Then there were the great cities Catherine
built, like the port of Odessa, where skilled

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:55.660
immigrants were invited in to transform the
economy.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:58.210
She even undertook the first detailed mapping
of Russia.

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:02.540
There’s a great story that Catherine was
in a meeting where a far-flung town was discussed.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:05.909
When Catherine asked where it was, all her
advisors shrugged.

00:17:05.909 --> 00:17:08.439
So she sent someone out to buy a map.

00:17:08.439 --> 00:17:12.419
When they came back, she discovered that not
even the map makers had bothered to map most

00:17:12.419 --> 00:17:13.419
of Russia.

00:17:13.419 --> 00:17:15.970
You better believe that changed pretty sharpish.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:19.899
But while Catherine’s reforms brought Russia
into line with its European neighbors, they

00:17:19.899 --> 00:17:20.949
weren’t all successful.

00:17:20.949 --> 00:17:24.740
Remember how her Instruction called for the
country’s 8 million serfs to be considered

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:25.740
people?

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:30.710
Those serfs were basically slaves, tied to
the land and owned by local landlords.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:34.529
When Catherine realized her core support came
from those same landlords, she did an about

00:17:34.529 --> 00:17:36.789
face and actually increased serfdom.

00:17:36.789 --> 00:17:41.179
This touches on the other issue of who benefited
from Catherine’s reforms.

00:17:41.179 --> 00:17:44.799
We mentioned earlier that 18th Century Russia
was about 95% rural.

00:17:44.799 --> 00:17:48.529
Yet the vast majority of Catherine’s reforms
were in urban areas.

00:17:48.529 --> 00:17:53.159
As transformative as her domestic agenda was,
millions of Russians didn’t even realize

00:17:53.159 --> 00:17:54.159
it was happening.

00:17:54.159 --> 00:17:58.340
Still, her reforms got her noticed abroad,
where people like Voltaire championed her

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.399
as a great philosopher empress.

00:18:00.399 --> 00:18:04.669
But Catherine’s reputation would soon become
international news again for a very different

00:18:04.669 --> 00:18:05.669
reason.

00:18:05.669 --> 00:18:09.210
It’s time to talk about sex.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:15.419
Love &amp; Death
So far in this video, we’ve met three of

00:18:15.419 --> 00:18:19.669
Catherine’s lovers: the guy who possibly
fathered Paul; the guy who became King of

00:18:19.669 --> 00:18:23.669
Poland; and Orlov, the guy who probably strangled
Peter III.

00:18:23.669 --> 00:18:26.700
Now it’s time to meet the most-famous of
them all.

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:30.830
Grigory Potemkin had been a background character
in Catherine’s life for decades.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:34.169
He’d helped Orlov organize the coup that
overthrew Peter III.

00:18:34.169 --> 00:18:38.159
In the Russo-Turkish War, he’d been the
one who secured Crimea.

00:18:38.159 --> 00:18:43.029
And now he was back in the imperial court,
determined to charm his way into power.

00:18:43.029 --> 00:18:46.950
To say Catherine and Potemkin were suited
to one another is to miss just how perfectly

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:47.999
they matched.

00:18:47.999 --> 00:18:50.269
Potemkin was ambitious, fearless.

00:18:50.269 --> 00:18:54.850
Not exactly handsome, but attractive in a
“this guy does not give two flying flips”

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:55.850
sort of way.

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:57.919
His ambition also complemented Catherine’s.

00:18:57.919 --> 00:19:02.679
While Catherine was happy to lose herself
in details, Potemkin was a big picture thinker,

00:19:02.679 --> 00:19:05.470
able to spur her dreams to greater heights.

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:07.720
Catherine summoned him to court in 1774.

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:10.230
Before the year was out, they were lovers.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:14.320
There’s even some evidence to suggest they
married in a secret ceremony, inside a tiny,

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.279
backwater church with only two witnesses.

00:19:16.279 --> 00:19:20.929
It’s certainly true she named him Prince
Potemkin, and elevated him to a status nearly

00:19:20.929 --> 00:19:22.289
equal to co-ruler.

00:19:22.289 --> 00:19:26.370
But while their burning ambition is what brought
Catherine and Potemkin together, it was also

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:28.179
what killed their love.

00:19:28.179 --> 00:19:32.570
By 1780, both were fully aware that their
love of power was corrupting their romantic

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:33.580
relationship.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:34.669
So they made a deal.

00:19:34.669 --> 00:19:37.619
From now on, Potemkin would remain as Catherine’s
political advisor.

00:19:37.619 --> 00:19:42.331
But in the bedroom, he would be replaced - not
with another ambitious, powerful man, but

00:19:42.331 --> 00:19:44.610
with a string of anonymous studs.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:50.370
And so we come to the one thing about Catherine
the Great that everyone remembers: her sexual

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:51.370
escapades.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:54.850
The fact this is monstrously unfair goes without
saying.

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:59.129
Catherine’s contemporary, Louis XV, had
so many mistresses he could give Prince a

00:19:59.129 --> 00:20:00.419
run for his money.

00:20:00.419 --> 00:20:02.660
Selim III had an actual harem.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:07.789
But, of course, it’s the highly-sexed woman
who goes down in history as using a horse

00:20:07.789 --> 00:20:12.590
to satisfy herself, as story as fake now as
it was then.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:14.779
Stay classy, 18th Century propagandists.

00:20:14.779 --> 00:20:18.880
Still, there’s no denying that Catherine’s
reputation abroad really did take a beating

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:23.970
in the back end of her reign, and not just
because of ill-founded sex rumors.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:28.700
In 1789, that revolution France had been trying
to avoid finally broke out, based on a lot

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:32.479
of those same Enlightenment ideals Catherine
professed to love.

00:20:32.479 --> 00:20:36.450
But now that those ideals were questioning
the divine right of monarchs, Catherine was

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:39.330
suddenly all like “What Enlightenment?

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:40.429
Voltaire?

00:20:40.429 --> 00:20:41.429
Never heard of him.”

00:20:41.429 --> 00:20:46.340
When Poland began agitating for a French-style
liberal constitution in 1792, she forced the

00:20:46.340 --> 00:20:51.440
Second Partition on the kingdom, dividing
most of it up between herself, Austria, and

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:52.440
Prussia.

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:56.809
When Tadeusz Kościuszko led a rebellion against
the partition two years later, Catherine cracked

00:20:56.809 --> 00:20:58.820
down as hard as any tyrant.

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:04.440
In Warsaw, some 20,000 Poles were executed
in a single day - more people than died during

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:06.539
the entire Reign of Terror.

00:21:06.539 --> 00:21:11.150
By the time Catherine instigated the Third
Partition of Poland in 1795, wiping the nation

00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:15.389
off the map, her Enlightenment credentials
had been reduced to a hollow punchline.

00:21:15.389 --> 00:21:17.870
But by then it barely mattered.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:22.340
Grigory Potemkin had died in 1791, leaving
Catherine isolated and lonely.

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:26.960
In his place, the empress had begun to devote
all her attention to her grandson Alexander,

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:29.780
even hinting that she would pass her son Paul
over for succession.

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:34.019
Naturally, this created a deep rift in the
imperial family, and left Paul eager to get

00:21:34.019 --> 00:21:35.740
one over on his mother.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:39.419
On November 5, 1796, Catherine was found on
the floor of her bathroom, almost carried

00:21:39.419 --> 00:21:40.610
off by a stroke.

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:45.139
The very next day she died, her grandson Alexander
still too young to take over from her.

00:21:45.139 --> 00:21:47.529
In his place, Paul I ascended to the throne.

00:21:47.529 --> 00:21:50.619
And he quickly set about trashing his mother’s
legacy.

00:21:50.619 --> 00:21:55.130
The first thing he did was have all her papers
burned, just in case she’d written him out

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:56.130
her will.

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:59.940
Then he had poor Peter Ulrich dug back up
and given the state funeral Catherine had

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:00.940
denied him.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:04.159
Finally, he ordered his mother buried alongside
his father.

00:22:04.159 --> 00:22:08.270
After spending the early part of their lives
entwined in a toxic marriage, Catherine would

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:11.610
now spend eternity alongside her hated husband,
too.

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:16.549
At the moment of her death, Catherine must’ve
wondered how history would remember her.

00:22:16.549 --> 00:22:21.869
Sure, she’d expanded Russian territory,
adding around 520,000 square kilometers.

00:22:21.869 --> 00:22:25.780
Sure, she’d brought in liberal reforms and
modernized urban Russia.

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:30.570
But, at the same time, she’d failed in her
goal to abolish serfdom, just as she’d failed

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:35.940
to institute reforms that improved life for
the 95% of the population who lived outside

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:36.940
the cities.

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:39.850
So why, then, do we remember her as Catherine
the Great?

00:22:39.850 --> 00:22:42.570
Well, from our modern perspective, it’s
easy to see why.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:47.279
Just listen to her life story: a provincial
girl who overcame a parental neglect, a forced

00:22:47.279 --> 00:22:51.289
marriage and an abusive husband to become
one of the most powerful women in Europe.

00:22:51.289 --> 00:22:55.100
But even in the 18th Century, it was clear
that Catherine was someone special.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:23:00.360
With her liberal reforms, she opened the door
for a Russia that existed to help its citizens,

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:01.649
not brutalize them.

00:23:01.649 --> 00:23:05.779
While that door would be slammed shut again
by successive regimes, knowledge of what lay

00:23:05.779 --> 00:23:08.149
behind it never really went away.

00:23:08.149 --> 00:23:09.650
All those utopian revolutionaries.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.840
All those moments of thaw in the 20th Century.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:16.019
Those shuddering moves towards openness in
the 1980s.

00:23:16.019 --> 00:23:21.559
All were distant descendants of Catherine’s
attempts to mould her country in a kinder

00:23:21.559 --> 00:23:22.559
image.

00:23:22.559 --> 00:23:26.570
She may not have always succeeded in her goals,
but Catherine deserves to be remembered by

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:27.879
her title.

00:23:27.879 --> 00:24:01.940
She really was the greatest empress in Russian
history.

